1f:T549,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BlogPosting","@id":"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/commuter-puzzle-two-minute/#article","mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/commuter-puzzle-two-minute"},"headline":"The commuter puzzle: making room for a two-minute game","description":"A field guide to designing for interrupted attention, small screens, and the lovely gap between “I have no time” and “I have two minutes.”","image":["https://www.outbrick.site/blog/commuter-puzzle-two-minute.png"],"datePublished":"2026-09-04","dateModified":"2026-09-04","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Mourad Hamdi","description":"Mourad makes small, thoughtful games for the space between one task and the next. OutBrick is his ongoing experiment in calmer play."},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"OutBrick","url":"https://www.outbrick.site","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.outbrick.site/icon.png"}},"articleSection":"OutBrick practice","keywords":"short sessions, commuter games, mobile design, puzzle games","citation":["https://doi.org/10.2196/46177","https://doi.org/10.1037/a0019440","https://doi.org/10.1177/0018720817715360","https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.220411","https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.241174"]}15:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$1f"}}]
16:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"OutBrick\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Journal\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site/blog\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"The commuter puzzle: making room for a two-minute game\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/commuter-puzzle-two-minute\"}]}"}}]
17:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How long is an OutBrick board?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"The core is designed to fit a short pause, but completion time varies with the board and the player. There is no timer in the main journey.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Can I leave a board unfinished?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Yes. OutBrick is designed to preserve your place and make returning understandable, so interruption does not turn play into a loss.\"}}]}"}}]
18:["$","section","design-for-the-interruption",{"id":"design-for-the-interruption","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Design for the interruption"}],[["$","p","A commuter game is played around announcements, doors, messages, and the simple fact that the player may need to look up. The first design question is therefore not “how do we maximize immersion?” but “can the player understand where they are when attention returns?”",{"children":"A commuter game is played around announcements, doors, messages, and the simple fact that the player may need to look up. The first design question is therefore not “how do we maximize immersion?” but “can the player understand where they are when attention returns?”"}],["$","p","Readable state is a form of care. A clear board, stable controls, and a visible pause point reduce the memory tax that comes with being interrupted.",{"children":"Readable state is a form of care. A clear board, stable controls, and a visible pause point reduce the memory tax that comes with being interrupted."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","puzzle-pilot-2023",{"href":"#reference-puzzle-pilot-2023","aria-label":"Jump to source: Urwyler et al. (2023)","children":["[",1,"]"]}],["$","a","gaming-fit-2025",{"href":"#reference-gaming-fit-2025","aria-label":"Jump to source: Ballou et al. (2025)","children":["[",5,"]"]}]]]}]]}]
19:["$","section","two-minutes-can-still-have-a-shape",{"id":"two-minutes-can-still-have-a-shape","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Two minutes can still have a shape"}],[["$","p","A short session should have a beginning, a middle, and an end. The beginning is recognition: what is the puzzle asking? The middle is a choice: which relationship should I test? The end is a result: what changed, and do I want to continue?",{"children":"A short session should have a beginning, a middle, and an end. The beginning is recognition: what is the puzzle asking? The middle is a choice: which relationship should I test? The end is a result: what changed, and do I want to continue?"}],["$","p","That shape is why the game can feel substantial without being long. The player receives a complete unit of attention instead of a fragment cut out of a larger obligation.",{"children":"That shape is why the game can feel substantial without being long. The player receives a complete unit of attention instead of a fragment cut out of a larger obligation."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","motivation-2010",{"href":"#reference-motivation-2010","aria-label":"Jump to source: Przybylski, Rigby, & Ryan (2010)","children":["[",2,"]"]}],["$","a","restoration-2017",{"href":"#reference-restoration-2017","aria-label":"Jump to source: Rupp et al. (2017)","children":["[",3,"]"]}]]]}]]}]
1a:["$","section","what-the-pilot-can-tell-us",{"id":"what-the-pilot-can-tell-us","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"What a pilot can—and cannot—tell us"}],[["$","p","Urwyler and colleagues studied a tablet-based puzzle-game intervention in a pilot feasibility randomized controlled trial with healthy adults. A pilot can help researchers understand recruitment, adherence, measurement, and whether a larger study is practical. It cannot establish that every short puzzle improves cognition or well-being.",{"children":"Urwyler and colleagues studied a tablet-based puzzle-game intervention in a pilot feasibility randomized controlled trial with healthy adults. A pilot can help researchers understand recruitment, adherence, measurement, and whether a larger study is practical. It cannot establish that every short puzzle improves cognition or well-being."}],["$","p","That distinction is useful for product teams. We can borrow a research question—does the experience fit the day, and can players use it consistently?—without borrowing a conclusion the study did not make.",{"children":"That distinction is useful for product teams. We can borrow a research question—does the experience fit the day, and can players use it consistently?—without borrowing a conclusion the study did not make."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","puzzle-pilot-2023",{"href":"#reference-puzzle-pilot-2023","aria-label":"Jump to source: Urwyler et al. (2023)","children":["[",1,"]"]}]]]}]]}]
1b:["$","section","outbrick-in-the-gap",{"id":"outbrick-in-the-gap","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"OutBrick in the gap"}],[["$","p","OutBrick is built for the gap between obligations. The board can be read quickly, a move can be undone without cost, and completion does not require a long chain of setup. If the train arrives or the coffee is ready, the player can leave with the board’s state intact.",{"children":"OutBrick is built for the gap between obligations. The board can be read quickly, a move can be undone without cost, and completion does not require a long chain of setup. If the train arrives or the coffee is ready, the player can leave with the board’s state intact."}],["$","p","The result is not productivity disguised as play. It is simply a small game that understands the shape of a real day—and gives the player permission to enjoy the gap without asking the gap to become something bigger.",{"children":"The result is not productivity disguised as play. It is simply a small game that understands the shape of a real day—and gives the player permission to enjoy the gap without asking the gap to become something bigger."}]],["$","ul",null,{"className":"article-bullets","children":[["$","li","Start with a board that explains itself",{"children":"Start with a board that explains itself"}],["$","li","Make interruption safe",{"children":"Make interruption safe"}],["$","li","Give a natural stopping point",{"children":"Give a natural stopping point"}],["$","li","Let a short session remain recreational",{"children":"Let a short session remain recreational"}]]}],null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","puzzle-pilot-2023",{"href":"#reference-puzzle-pilot-2023","aria-label":"Jump to source: Urwyler et al. (2023)","children":["[",1,"]"]}],["$","a","time-wellbeing-2022",{"href":"#reference-time-wellbeing-2022","aria-label":"Jump to source: Vuorre et al. (2022)","children":["[",4,"]"]}],["$","a","gaming-fit-2025",{"href":"#reference-gaming-fit-2025","aria-label":"Jump to source: Ballou et al. (2025)","children":["[",5,"]"]}]]]}]]}]
1c:["$","section",null,{"id":"faq","className":"article-faq","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"blog-kicker","children":"Questions, answered"}],["$","h2",null,{"children":"A few useful answers"}],[["$","details","How long is an OutBrick board?",{"children":[["$","summary",null,{"children":"How long is an OutBrick board?"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The core is designed to fit a short pause, but completion time varies with the board and the player. There is no timer in the main journey."}]]}],["$","details","Can I leave a board unfinished?",{"children":[["$","summary",null,{"children":"Can I leave a board unfinished?"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Yes. OutBrick is designed to preserve your place and make returning understandable, so interruption does not turn play into a loss."}]]}]]]}]
